---
// blog/[slug].astro - Dedicated Blog Article Reader
import MarketingLayout from '../../layouts/MarketingLayout.astro';
import { ArrowLeft, ArrowRight } from '@lucide/astro';

export function getStaticPaths() {
  const rustForProjectIntentArticle = {
    title: "Rust for Project Intent: How AFWE Caught Its Own Architectural Blindspots",
    subtitle: "Rust made memory mistakes impossible to make quietly. AFWE makes regressions of intent impossible to make quietly. Inside the dogfooding post-mortem that proved how silent capability severance happens—and the deterministic ratchets that stop it.",
    date: 'October 27, 2026',
    author: 'AFWE Core Architecture Team',
    category: 'Architecture & Governance',
    readTime: '10 min read',
    content: `
      <p class="text-base sm:text-lg leading-relaxed text-graphite/90 dark:text-stone-200 font-sans">
        <em>"Rust for project intent."</em> Rust made memory mistakes impossible to make quietly. AFWE makes regressions of intent impossible to make quietly. <strong>"Quietly"</strong> is the word that keeps the claim honest: you can still break things on purpose, but you can never break them by accident.
      </p>

      <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-graphite/90 dark:text-stone-200 mt-4">
        Last week, AFWE had an uncomfortable moment. During our own dogfooding iterations on branch <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-stone-200 dark:bg-obsidian-800 font-mono text-xs">ReBorn</code>, an autonomous agent retired legacy v1 workflows in Turn <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-stone-200 dark:bg-obsidian-800 font-mono text-xs">t0022</code>. In doing so, it removed the caller to <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-stone-200 dark:bg-obsidian-800 font-mono text-xs">contract::task_start</code> in the CLI crate, but <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-stone-200 dark:bg-obsidian-800 font-mono text-xs">crates/afwe-core/src/contract.rs</code> was never touched.
      </p>

      <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-graphite/90 dark:text-stone-200 mt-4">
        The function went completely dead. At the exact same time, duplicate briefing logic was written into <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-stone-200 dark:bg-obsidian-800 font-mono text-xs">turn.begin</code> instead of reusing <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-stone-200 dark:bg-obsidian-800 font-mono text-xs">context::scope_matches</code>. AFWE's commit gate committed cleanly. The tool built to stop silent architectural decay had failed to catch dead code and duplication in its own engine.
      </p>

      <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-graphite/90 dark:text-stone-200 mt-4">
        Rather than paper over the failure with prompt engineering, we stopped development and initiated an exhaustive post-mortem. This article documents what broke, the fierce debates and pushbacks during design, and the multi-level deterministic ratchets that now guarantee this failure mode is physically impossible.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-graphite dark:text-white mt-10 mb-4">1. The Blindspot: Why Checking Touched Files Misses the Real World</h2>
      <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-graphite/90 dark:text-stone-200">
        Most static analysis and code verification systems operate under a flawed assumption: <em>inspect only the files changed in the pull request</em>.
      </p>
      <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-graphite/90 dark:text-stone-200 mt-4">
        In our Turn <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-stone-200 dark:bg-obsidian-800 font-mono text-xs">t0022</code> incident, the agent edited <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-stone-200 dark:bg-obsidian-800 font-mono text-xs">crates/afwe-cli/src/main.rs</code>. It deleted the call site of <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-stone-200 dark:bg-obsidian-800 font-mono text-xs">contract::task_start</code>. Because <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-stone-200 dark:bg-obsidian-800 font-mono text-xs">crates/afwe-core/src/contract.rs</code> had 0 git diff bytes, the commit gate never analyzed <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-stone-200 dark:bg-obsidian-800 font-mono text-xs">contract.rs</code>.
      </p>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-300 dark:border-obsidian-700 bg-stone-100 dark:bg-obsidian-850 p-4 font-mono text-xs my-4 text-graphite dark:text-stone-300">
        Turn t0022 git diff:<br/>
        &nbsp;&nbsp;M crates/afwe-cli/src/main.rs&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;[-contract::task_start(...)]<br/>
        &nbsp;&nbsp;[crates/afwe-core/src/contract.rs]&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;[UNTOUCHED — 0 bytes diff]<br/>
        Gate verdict: PASSED (Blindspot: callee was severed in an untouched file)
      </div>
      <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-graphite/90 dark:text-stone-200 mt-4">
        A function almost never goes dead because someone deleted the function itself; it goes dead because someone removed its caller, which sits in a completely different file. Checking only touched files makes cross-module severance completely invisible.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-graphite dark:text-white mt-10 mb-4">2. The Design Debate: Concessions, Pushbacks, and Realities</h2>
      <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-graphite/90 dark:text-stone-200">
        When fixing this blindspot, we initially proposed tracking dead code down to every struct field, enum variant, and cross-file import edge. That proposal triggered severe pushback from our core architects:
      </p>

      <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-3 mt-4 text-sm font-sans text-graphite/80 dark:text-stone-300">
        <li>
          <strong>The "0% False Positives" Overclaim</strong>: 
          Pure import-edge diffing fails on dynamic dispatch (<code class="px-1 font-mono text-xs">engine.run()</code>), trait objects, glob imports (<code class="px-1 font-mono text-xs">use contract::*</code>), and macro registrations. A tree-sitter AST parser cannot statically prove which <code class="px-1 font-mono text-xs">run()</code> was invoked without full compiler type resolution.
          <em>The resolution:</em> We over-approximate edges. Any identifier occurrence anywhere in code counts as an inbound reference. Errors only ever go in the safe direction: we may miss an unused function with a generic name, but we will <strong>never falsely block live code</strong>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Conceding Struct Fields & Enum Variants to the Compiler</strong>:
          Struct fields and enum variants are deeply affected by serialization frameworks (Serde, Jackson, Pydantic). A struct field might only ever be populated via JSON deserialization and never constructed directly in code. Attempting to track fields statically produces intolerable false alarms. Furthermore, <code class="px-1 font-mono text-xs">rustc -D dead_code</code> already catches private unused items. We conceded this domain to the compiler.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Grandfathering Pre-Existing Debt</strong>:
          If you run a dead-code gate on an established repository, it will flag hundreds of existing legacy functions on Day 1. Developers experience alert fatigue and disable the tool.
          <em>The resolution:</em> AFWE ratchets against a baseline. Pre-existing zero-reference symbols are grandfathered in <code class="px-1 font-mono text-xs">baseline.yaml</code>. The gate strictly blocks newly severed capabilities (<code class="px-1 font-mono text-xs">&gt;0 &rarr; 0</code>) and functions born dead in touched files.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Tamper-Resistant Root Inference</strong>:
          If an agent can arbitrarily add dead functions to an "exempt roots" list in the manifest to silence the gate, the ratchet is gamed. We enforce automatic root inference (<code class="px-1 font-mono text-xs">main.rs</code>, <code class="px-1 font-mono text-xs">lib.rs</code>, <code class="px-1 font-mono text-xs">index.ts</code>, <code class="px-1 font-mono text-xs">tests/**</code>) and treat manual root edits like pin modifications—requiring an explicit override reason.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-graphite dark:text-white mt-10 mb-4">3. The Final Solution: A Two-Prong Architecture</h2>
      <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-graphite/90 dark:text-stone-200">
        The resulting implementation divides architectural governance into two high-leverage engines:
      </p>

      <div class="my-6 border border-stone-300 dark:border-obsidian-700 rounded-2xl p-6 bg-stone-50 dark:bg-obsidian-900 space-y-6">
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-graphite dark:text-white">Prong 1: Decision-to-Code Link Ratchet (AFWE's Unique Superpower)</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-graphite/80 dark:text-stone-300 mt-2 leading-relaxed">
            Existing linters only analyze code syntax; they know nothing of human architectural intent. In AFWE, active architectural decisions (<code class="px-1 font-mono text-xs">.afwe/memory/**/*.md</code>), pinned invariants (<code class="px-1 font-mono text-xs">.afwe/pins/*.yaml</code>), and documentation link directly to blueprint nodes, files, and symbols.
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc list-inside mt-2 text-xs font-mono text-graphite/70 dark:text-stone-400 space-y-1">
            <li><strong>ORPHANED_DECISION</strong>: Blocks if a symbol, file, or node anchored to an active decision vanishes without an explicit supersede or update.</li>
            <li><strong>ORPHANED_PIN</strong>: Blocks if a pinned invariant loses its implementing code.</li>
            <li><strong>DOCS_DRIFT</strong>: Blocks if markdown documentation links point to deleted code files.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="border-t border-stone-200 dark:border-obsidian-800 pt-6">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-graphite dark:text-white">Prong 2: Minimal Cross-Boundary Inbound-Reference Ratchet</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-graphite/80 dark:text-stone-300 mt-2 leading-relaxed">
            A single-pass ASCII identifier scanner tokenizes all project files in under 15 milliseconds. It compares workspace-wide reference counts between <code class="px-1 font-mono text-xs">turn begin</code> and <code class="px-1 font-mono text-xs">turn commit</code>.
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc list-inside mt-2 text-xs font-mono text-graphite/70 dark:text-stone-400 space-y-1">
            <li><strong>SEVERED_CAPABILITY</strong>: Flags when an exported symbol drops from &gt;0 to 0 inbound references, catching cross-module caller deletions even if the callee file was never modified!</li>
            <li><strong>DEAD_ON_ARRIVAL</strong>: Flags newly introduced exported symbols born with 0 references in touched files, forcing agents to either wire them up, test them in <code class="px-1 font-mono text-xs">tests/</code>, or declare them in turn intents.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="rounded-xl border-l-4 border-amber-500 bg-amber-500/10 p-5 my-8 space-y-2">
        <div class="text-xs font-mono font-bold tracking-wider uppercase text-amber-600 dark:text-amber-400">
          IMPORTANT: Over-Approximation Policy & Guarantees
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-graphite dark:text-stone-200 font-sans leading-relaxed">
          <strong>Over-Approximation Policy (Safe Error Direction):</strong> To prevent false alarms on dynamic dispatch, macros, or string lookups, the identifier index over-approximates references. Any identifier token occurrence in code counts as a reference.
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-xs font-sans text-graphite/80 dark:text-stone-300 space-y-1">
          <li><strong>The Guarantee:</strong> AFWE will never falsely block live code.</li>
          <li><strong>The Trade-off:</strong> Generic names (<code class="px-1 font-mono">new</code>, <code class="px-1 font-mono">len</code>, <code class="px-1 font-mono">init</code>, <code class="px-1 font-mono">run</code>, <code class="px-1 font-mono">default</code>, <code class="px-1 font-mono">get</code>, <code class="px-1 font-mono">set</code>) are intentionally treated as always referenced and unflaggable.</li>
          <li><strong>Language-Adaptive:</strong> Weakly typed languages (Python/JS) leverage identifier token matching across files; typed languages (Rust/Go/TS) leverage module-scoped import specifiers where available.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-graphite dark:text-white mt-10 mb-4">4. Proving the Fix: The Interactive Demo</h2>
      <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-graphite/90 dark:text-stone-200">
        To test these capabilities in your own terminal, we updated the official AFWE test harness with dedicated cross-module capability tests. You can run the interactive walkthrough in bash or PowerShell:
      </p>
      <pre class="bg-graphite text-stone-200 dark:bg-obsidian-900 p-4 rounded-xl font-mono text-xs overflow-x-auto my-4">
# On Linux / macOS
./scripts/demo.sh

# On Windows (PowerShell)
.\scripts\demo.ps1
      </pre>

      <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-graphite/90 dark:text-stone-200 mt-4">
        During Step 3 of the demo, the script simulates the exact <code class="px-1 font-mono text-xs">t0022</code> bug: it modifies <code class="px-1 font-mono text-xs">checkout.ts</code> and removes the call to <code class="px-1 font-mono text-xs">charge2</code>, leaving <code class="px-1 font-mono text-xs">charge.ts</code> untouched. Watch the commit gate intercept the regression:
      </p>

      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-300 dark:border-obsidian-700 bg-stone-100 dark:bg-obsidian-850 p-4 font-mono text-xs my-4 text-graphite dark:text-stone-300">
        &gt; afwe turn commit t0003<br/>
        &bull; REDO &mdash; t0003 NOT committed<br/>
        &nbsp;&nbsp;&#10006; SEVERED_CAPABILITY: &apos;src/payments/charge.ts::charge2&apos; lost all incoming callers (dropped from 1 to 0) &mdash; restore caller, wire it in, or declare it in turn assume with action remove or replace<br/>
        &rarr; fix the failures above, then call turn.commit again. Nothing was committed.
      </div>

      <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-graphite/90 dark:text-stone-200 mt-4">
        The gate refuses to commit until the engineer or agent explicitly declares the intent:
      </p>
      <pre class="bg-graphite text-stone-200 dark:bg-obsidian-900 p-4 rounded-xl font-mono text-xs overflow-x-auto my-4">
# In assume.json:
{
  "intents": [
    { "id": "decouple-charge", "action": "update", "targets": ["payments"] },
    { "id": "deprecate-charge2", "action": "replace", "targets": ["src/payments/charge.ts"] }
  ]
}
      </pre>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-graphite dark:text-white mt-10 mb-4">5. Conclusion: What "Quietly" Really Means</h2>
      <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-graphite/90 dark:text-stone-200">
        Rust does not prevent you from using <code class="px-1 font-mono text-xs">unsafe</code> to dereference a raw pointer when you genuinely need to write low-level hardware drivers. What Rust guarantees is that you cannot dereference a null pointer <em>quietly</em>. The compiler forces you to write <code class="px-1 font-mono text-xs">unsafe { ... }</code>, recording the trade-off explicitly in source code.
      </p>
      <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-graphite/90 dark:text-stone-200 mt-4">
        AFWE does the same for architectural intent. If you want to retire a capability, delete a module, or break an architectural boundary, AFWE does not stop you. But you cannot do it quietly. You cannot do it because an LLM hallucinated, lost context, or forgot what was agreed upon three turns ago. You must declare the intent in <code class="px-1 font-mono text-xs">turn assume</code>, record the rationale, and sign the commit.
      </p>
      <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-graphite/90 dark:text-stone-200 mt-4 font-semibold">
        You can break things on purpose. You can never break them by accident.
      </p>
    `
  };

  const sponsoredSeatPoolArticle = {
    title: "The Sponsored Seat Pool: Funding AFWE Without Feature Gates",
    subtitle: "How sponsored distribution, paid recognition and community points can sustain an open-source product without splitting its core capabilities into paid tiers.",
    date: 'October 26, 2026',
    author: 'AFWE Core Architecture Team',
    category: 'Economics & Open Source',
    readTime: '7 min read',
    content: `
      <p class="text-base sm:text-lg leading-relaxed text-graphite/90 dark:text-stone-200 font-sans">
        Open-source developer tools face an essential challenge: software requires sustained maintenance, but separating developer ergonomics into paid tiers often fragments the community. Today, two legitimate approaches have emerged to solve this dilemma.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-graphite dark:text-white mt-10 mb-4">1. The Principle: Two Approaches to Open-Source Sustainability</h2>
      <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-graphite/90 dark:text-stone-200">
        The first model is <strong>paid extensions and support</strong>. Respected projects like React Flow sustain an open-source core under the MIT license while offering Pro subscriptions that include production-ready application templates, specialized examples, prioritized issue handling, and direct technical support. For UI canvas libraries and component ecosystems, this provides clear value to enterprises while keeping the baseline library open for everyone.
      </p>
      <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-graphite/90 dark:text-stone-200 mt-4">
        The second model is <strong>AFWE's proposed recognition-led model</strong>. In this design, AFWE keeps its entire capability set completely identical for every user, funding official packaged distribution through sponsored-seat allocations while offering commercial tiers focused on supporter recognition, brand customization, and community roadmap prioritization.
      </p>
      <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-graphite/90 dark:text-stone-200 mt-4">
        We chose this recognition-led approach because of the specific problem AFWE solves: architectural governance. When AI agents write code, the guardrails, tree-sitter AST validation checks, and boundary rules that keep software from deteriorating must be uniform across every developer on a team. If verification checks or visual radar tools were restricted to high-tier subscriptions, individual contributors and open-source collaborators would work without them, accelerating architectural drift. For an architectural tool to succeed, everyone must run the exact same verification engine.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-graphite dark:text-white mt-10 mb-4">2. The Distribution Model: Capacity Without Restrictions</h2>
      <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-graphite/90 dark:text-stone-200">
        In our proposed distribution model, official precompiled binaries hosted on our website are backed by a <strong>Community-Sponsored Seat Pool</strong>. Here is what a sponsored seat operationalizes:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 mt-4 text-sm font-sans text-graphite/80 dark:text-stone-300">
        <li><strong>Operational Definition</strong>: One seat represents one funded allocation for an active installation or user utilizing our official packaged distribution.</li>
        <li><strong>Target Funding Model</strong>: Providing signed cross-platform releases, automated multi-platform builds, fast download CDN hosting, tree-sitter grammar updates, and community triage incurs real operational expenses. We calculated targeted monthly contributions per funded seat across three supporter tiers:
          <ul class="list-disc list-inside ml-6 mt-2 space-y-1 text-xs font-mono">
            <li>Presence ($29/month): Funds 50 community seats (~$0.58 per seat).</li>
            <li>Palette ($79/month): Funds 150 community seats (~$0.53 per seat).</li>
            <li>Motion ($199/month): Funds 500 community seats (~$0.40 per seat).</li>
          </ul>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-graphite/90 dark:text-stone-200 mt-4">
        When open capacity exists in the pool, any developer can download the official precompiled binary with one click. If the open seat pool ever reaches zero, direct packaged downloads from the website will temporarily pause until new capacity is sponsored.
      </p>
      <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-graphite/90 dark:text-stone-200 mt-4">
        Crucially, this is a limit on our official packaged distribution, not on the software itself. AFWE is 100% open source under the MIT License. Anyone can clone the repository and compile the binaries locally using <code class="px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded bg-stone-200 dark:bg-obsidian-800 font-mono text-xs">cargo build</code> at any time, with zero limits.
      </p>
      <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-graphite/90 dark:text-stone-200 mt-4">
        Regarding telemetry, seat allocations count discrete seat claims without gathering behavioral analytics. AFWE does not track user behavior, does not scan codebases, and does not upload proprietary source files.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-graphite dark:text-white mt-10 mb-4">3. The Recognition Model: The Sustainer Gate</h2>
      <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-graphite/90 dark:text-stone-200">
        The Sustainer Gate is AFWE's community recognition mechanism. On first launch, an installation displays a recognition card attributing the release to an active sustainer, accompanied by a subtle update to AFWE default styling that highlights the supporter.
      </p>
      <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-graphite/90 dark:text-stone-200 mt-4">
        The system separates two clear choices:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 mt-4 text-sm font-sans text-graphite/80 dark:text-stone-300">
        <li><strong>The Sustainer's Choice</strong>: Supporters choose whether their branding is eligible to appear, and what visual styling they unlock across our four tiers:
          <ul class="list-disc list-inside ml-6 mt-2 space-y-1 text-xs font-mono">
            <li>Community (Free): Full product access, baseline monthly points, and local UI customization.</li>
            <li>Presence (Tier 01): Custom name, brand identity, and verified logo.</li>
            <li>Palette (Tier 02): Custom brand color palettes, rim styling, and badge design.</li>
            <li>Motion (Tier 03): Subtle micro-animations and advanced visual treatments.</li>
          </ul>
          When sustainers launch their own copy of AFWE, they see their own attribution card, replacing default branding.
        </li>
        <li><strong>The Installation Owner's Choice</strong>: The recognition experience is enabled by default to thank community sponsors, but any developer can disable it in settings with two clicks to return to the plain default interface.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-graphite/90 dark:text-stone-200 mt-4">
        Reliable fallback is built into the specification: if the network is offline or the routing service is temporarily unreachable, AFWE continues running with zero disruption and respects the user's saved preferences.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-graphite dark:text-white mt-10 mb-4">4. The Community Model: Points and Engineering Support</h2>
      <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-graphite/90 dark:text-stone-200">
        To guide project priorities transparently, AFWE introduces a monthly points allocation:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 mt-4 text-sm font-sans text-graphite/80 dark:text-stone-300">
        <li><strong>Baseline Allocation</strong>: Every developer with a claimed community account receives baseline monthly points.</li>
        <li><strong>3:1 Sustainer Ratio</strong>: Active sustainers receive a 3x multiplier on their monthly allocation. AFWE documents these rules publicly and may adjust multipliers under published guidelines.</li>
        <li><strong>Roadmap Support</strong>: Points can be applied to upvote roadmap proposals and signal community interest. Points inform our focus but do not guarantee immediate implementation; AFWE retains final technical responsibility for its architecture and release roadmap.</li>
        <li><strong>Open Support Knowledge</strong>: When our core team assists an organization with a complex architectural boundary or custom guardrail, that solution is sanitized of confidential details and published as an open blueprint for the entire community, subject to that organization's explicit permission.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-graphite dark:text-white mt-10 mb-4">5. Current Status and Next Steps</h2>
      <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-graphite/90 dark:text-stone-200">
        We believe in transparency about what is live versus what is planned:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 mt-4 text-sm font-sans text-graphite/80 dark:text-stone-300">
        <li><strong>Live Today</strong>: The core Rust engine, CLI, 57 Model Context Protocol tools, Tree-sitter AST validation, interactive Studio, and local UI customization are fully operational under the MIT license.</li>
        <li><strong>Planned (Phase 4 Community)</strong>: The automated Sustainer Gate routing mechanism, real-time website allocation limits, and the monthly points governance system are actively in development as proposed systems.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-graphite/90 dark:text-stone-200 mt-4">
        By connecting sustainability to voluntary recognition and community capacity rather than restrictive paywalls, we can ensure AFWE remains a dependable foundation for autonomous coding agents and human engineers alike.
      </p>
    `
  };

  return [
    {
      params: { slug: 'rust-for-project-intent' },
      props: rustForProjectIntentArticle
    },
    {
      params: { slug: 'the-sponsored-seat-pool' },
      props: sponsoredSeatPoolArticle
    },
    {
      params: { slug: 'why-afwe-rejects-feature-gatekeeping' },
      props: sponsoredSeatPoolArticle
    },
    {
      params: { slug: 'why-graphrag-isnt-enough' },
      props: {
        title: "Why GraphRAG Isn't Enough: Double-Entry Bookkeeping for Codebases",
        subtitle: "Vector databases and dependency graphs show what code is. They cannot tell what code was supposed to be.",
        date: 'October 24, 2026',
        author: 'AFWE Core Architecture Team',
        category: 'Architecture & Foundations',
        readTime: '6 min read',
        content: `
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-graphite/90 dark:text-stone-200">
            Current developer tooling relies on a single premise: if you feed an LLM enough relevant context snippets from the codebase, it will produce code that respects your architecture.
          </p>
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-graphite/90 dark:text-stone-200 mt-4">
            In practice, this model breaks down. Code search, AST query tools, and GraphRAG can tell an agent what your codebase <em>is</em> at this exact second. They cannot tell the agent what your code was <em>supposed to be</em>.
          </p>
          
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-graphite dark:text-white mt-8 mb-4">The Accounting Defect in AI Coding</h2>
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-graphite/90 dark:text-stone-200">
            For five hundred years, accountants have not relied on a single ledger. Single-entry bookkeeping is prone to silent fraud, missing entries, and drift. To prevent errors, double-entry bookkeeping requires every credit to balance an offsetting debit.
          </p>
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-graphite/90 dark:text-stone-200 mt-4">
            AFWE applies double-entry bookkeeping to software:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 mt-4 text-sm font-mono text-graphite/80 dark:text-stone-300">
            <li><strong>Ledger 1 (The Implementation)</strong>: The actual physical source code in your repository, parsed via Tree-sitter.</li>
            <li><strong>Ledger 2 (The Intent and Memory)</strong>: The declared AST claims, active pins, and geometric boundary constraints in <code class="px-1 py-0.5 rounded bg-stone-200 dark:bg-obsidian-800">.afwe/</code>.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-graphite dark:text-white mt-8 mb-4">Verification at the Commit Boundary</h2>
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-graphite/90 dark:text-stone-200">
            When an agent finishes editing files, AFWE does not ask an LLM if the change looks good. A deterministic Rust kernel checks every declared claim and invariant rule. If an agent silently dropped a checkout payment method or violated an architectural boundary, the turn gate returns a REDO with structured leaf-to-root diagnostics.
          </p>
        `
      }
    },
    {
      params: { slug: 'constraints-as-geometry' },
      props: {
        title: 'Constraints as Geometry: Active Gating vs Prose Suggestions',
        subtitle: "Why geometric graph constraints stop agent hallucinations at the pre-commit gate.",
        date: 'October 18, 2026',
        author: 'AFWE Core Architecture Team',
        category: 'Formal Verification',
        readTime: '8 min read',
        content: `
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-graphite/90 dark:text-stone-200">
            Most teams attempt to govern AI coding tools by writing long system prompts: <em>"Never import database models in presentation components"</em>, or <em>"Ensure payments remain decoupled from UI"</em>.
          </p>
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-graphite/90 dark:text-stone-200 mt-4">
            As conversations stretch and context windows fill up, agents reliably forget negative instructions. Prose instructions are advisory suggestions; compilers and git hooks require hard mathematics.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-graphite dark:text-white mt-8 mb-4">Directional AST Invariants</h2>
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-graphite/90 dark:text-stone-200">
            In AFWE, architecture rules are defined as directed graph edges in <code class="px-1 py-0.5 rounded bg-stone-200 dark:bg-obsidian-800">constraints.yaml</code>:
          </p>
          <pre class="bg-graphite text-stone-200 dark:bg-obsidian-900 p-4 rounded-xl font-mono text-xs overflow-x-auto my-4">
constraints:
  - from: payments
    must_not_depend: web-ui
    reason: "Payment logic must remain headless and testable in isolation"
          </pre>
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-graphite/90 dark:text-stone-200">
            If an agent introduces a forbidden import statement, the turn kernel immediately intercepts the violation during <code class="px-1 py-0.5 rounded bg-stone-200 dark:bg-obsidian-800 font-mono text-xs">afwe turn commit</code> before any commit is written to git.
          </p>
        `
      }
    },
    {
      params: { slug: 'blake3-tripwire-protocol' },
      props: {
        title: 'The BLAKE3 Tripwire: Tamper-Proof Guardrails in CI',
        subtitle: "Inside the cryptographic manifest that secures .afwe/ against silent tampering in pull requests.",
        date: 'October 12, 2026',
        author: 'AFWE Core Architecture Team',
        category: 'Security & CI',
        readTime: '5 min read',
        content: `
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-graphite/90 dark:text-stone-200">
            When autonomous coding agents are granted pull request rights, a critical security vulnerability arises: what prevents the agent from modifying the CI configuration or deleting the linter rules that caught its bugs?
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-graphite dark:text-white mt-8 mb-4">Cryptographic Guardrail Manifests</h2>
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-graphite/90 dark:text-stone-200">
            When you run <code class="px-1 py-0.5 rounded bg-stone-200 dark:bg-obsidian-800 font-mono text-xs">afwe materialize --apply</code>, AFWE computes a BLAKE3 cryptographic hash of every guardrail specification, constraint file, and pre-commit hook, storing them in <code class="px-1 py-0.5 rounded bg-stone-200 dark:bg-obsidian-800 font-mono text-xs">.afwe/guardrails/manifest.yaml</code>.
          </p>
          <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-graphite/90 dark:text-stone-200 mt-4">
            In GitHub Actions or GitLab CI, running <code class="px-1 py-0.5 rounded bg-stone-200 dark:bg-obsidian-800 font-mono text-xs">afwe materialize --check --base origin/main</code> compares the PR manifest directly against the protected branch. If an agent edited a rule file without a signed human confirmation turn, the CI gate blocks the merge automatically.
          </p>
        `
      }
    }
  ];
}

const { title, subtitle, date, author, category, readTime, content } = Astro.props;
---

<MarketingLayout title={`${title} · AFWE Blog`} activeNav="blog">
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-10 sm:py-16 space-y-10">
    <div class="space-y-4 border-b border-stone-300 dark:border-obsidian-800 pb-8">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 text-xs font-mono text-graphite/60 dark:text-stone-400">
        <a href="/blog" class="hover:underline">← Blog</a>
        <span>/</span>
        <span class="text-survey-600 dark:text-survey-light font-bold">{category}</span>
      </div>
      <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl lg:text-5xl font-bold text-graphite dark:text-white leading-[1.15] tracking-tight">
        {title}
      </h1>
      {subtitle && (
        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-graphite/75 dark:text-stone-300 font-sans leading-relaxed">
          {subtitle}
        </p>
      )}
      <div class="flex items-center gap-4 text-xs font-mono text-graphite/70 dark:text-stone-400 pt-2">
        <span>{author}</span>
        <span>•</span>
        <span>{date}</span>
        <span>•</span>
        <span>{readTime}</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose dark:prose-invert max-w-none font-sans" set:html={content} />

    <div class="pt-10 border-t border-stone-300 dark:border-obsidian-800 flex items-center justify-between font-mono text-xs">
      <a href="/blog" class="text-graphite dark:text-stone-200 hover:text-survey-600 dark:hover:text-survey-light font-semibold inline-flex items-center gap-1.5">
        <ArrowLeft class="size-3" />
        <span>Back to all articles</span>
      </a>
      <a href="/docs/getting-started/quick-start" class="text-survey-600 dark:text-survey-light hover:underline font-bold inline-flex items-center gap-1.5">
        <span>Try AFWE in 5 minutes</span>
        <ArrowRight class="size-3" />
      </a>
    </div>
  </article>
</MarketingLayout>
